(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-neuro-exam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Neurological Exam (Central & Peripheral Nervous System) — Set 2 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"On the 0-to-5 motor strength scale, what does a grade of 5 out of 5 indicate?","choices":["Active movement only with gravity eliminated","Active movement against resistance without fatigue","No muscle contraction detected at all","A barely detectable flicker of contraction"],"answer":1,"exp":"A motor strength grade of five out of five is active movement against resistance without evident fatigue — normal strength. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"A grade of 3 out of 5 motor strength corresponds to what?","choices":["No muscle contraction detected at all","Movement against gravity and full resistance","A barely detectable trace of contraction","Active movement against gravity but not resistance"],"answer":3,"exp":"A motor strength grade of three out of five is active movement against gravity but not against added resistance. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"Muscle tone is evaluated by assessing what?","choices":["The muscle's size and contour on inspection","The muscle's resistance to passive stretch","The strength of the muscle against resistance","The reflex response to a tendon tap"],"answer":1,"exp":"Muscle tone is the slight residual tension in a muscle, evaluated by assessing its resistance to passive stretch. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 37"},{"q":"Muscle bulk is assessed by doing what?","choices":["Assessing resistance to passive stretch","Inspecting the size and contour of the muscles","Grading strength against examiner resistance","Striking the tendon with a reflex hammer"],"answer":1,"exp":"Muscle bulk is assessed by inspecting the size and contour of the muscles, noting whether they are flat, concave, or atrophied, especially in the hands, shoulders, and thighs. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 37"},{"q":"Shoulder abduction by the deltoid is primarily innervated by which nerve roots?","choices":["L2 and L3 (femoral nerve)","S1 alone (tibial nerve)","C5 and C6 (axillary nerve)","T8 and T9 (intercostal nerves)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Shoulder abduction by the deltoid is primarily innervated by the C5 and C6 nerve roots through the axillary nerve. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 38"},{"q":"Ankle plantar flexion by the gastrocnemius and soleus is primarily innervated by which nerve root?","choices":["C7 (radial nerve)","L2 (femoral nerve)","C5 (axillary nerve)","S1 (tibial nerve)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Ankle plantar flexion by the gastrocnemius and soleus is primarily innervated by the S1 nerve root through the tibial nerve. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 39"},{"q":"Knee extension by the quadriceps is primarily innervated by which nerve roots?","choices":["C6, C7, and C8 (radial nerve)","S1 and S2 (sciatic nerve)","C8 and T1 (ulnar nerve)","L2, L3, and L4 (femoral nerve)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Knee extension by the quadriceps is primarily innervated by the L2, L3, and L4 nerve roots through the femoral nerve. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 39"},{"q":"The biceps deep tendon reflex tests primarily which nerve roots?","choices":["L2 and L3","S1 and S2","C5 and C6","T10 and T11"],"answer":2,"exp":"The biceps deep tendon reflex tests primarily the C5 and C6 nerve roots. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 50"},{"q":"The patellar (quadriceps) deep tendon reflex tests primarily which nerve roots?","choices":["L2, L3, and L4","C5 and C6","C6 and C7","S1 only"],"answer":0,"exp":"The patellar, or quadriceps, deep tendon reflex tests primarily the L2, L3, and L4 nerve roots. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 50"},{"q":"The Achilles (ankle) deep tendon reflex tests primarily which nerve root?","choices":["C7","L4","S1","T12"],"answer":2,"exp":"The Achilles, or ankle, deep tendon reflex tests primarily the S1 nerve root. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 50"},{"q":"On the reflex grading scale, what does a grade of 2 plus indicate?","choices":["Average, normal reflex activity","A hyperactive reflex with clonus","A diminished, low-normal reflex","No reflex response at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"A reflex graded two plus is average, normal activity; one plus is diminished, three plus is brisker than average, and four plus is hyperactive with clonus. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 52"},{"q":"A reflex graded 4 plus, very brisk with rhythmic oscillations, is described as having what?","choices":["Areflexia","Fasciculation","Clonus","Atrophy"],"answer":2,"exp":"A four plus reflex is very brisk and hyperactive with clonus — rhythmic oscillations between flexion and extension. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 52"},{"q":"When stroking the sole of the foot, the normal plantar (Babinski) response in an adult is what?","choices":["Upward extension of the great toe","Downward flexion of the toes","No movement of the foot at all","Rhythmic clonus at the ankle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The normal plantar response in an adult is downward flexion of the toes; an upgoing great toe is abnormal and reflects a corticospinal tract lesion. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"The plantar (Babinski) response evaluates the integrity of which pathway?","choices":["The corticospinal tract","The spinothalamic tract","The posterior columns","The vestibular system"],"answer":0,"exp":"The plantar (Babinski) response, tested at the L5 and S1 levels, evaluates the integrity of the corticospinal tract. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which distraction (reinforcement) technique can help elicit a lower-limb reflex that is hard to obtain?","choices":["Having the patient hold their breath fully","Having the patient close both eyes tightly","Having the patient clasp the hands and pull them apart","Having the patient stand on one leg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Distraction techniques such as clasping the hands and pulling against them (or clenching the jaw) can reinforce a hard-to-elicit reflex; a reflex needing reinforcement is graded one. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 58"},{"q":"Pain and temperature sensation are carried primarily by which pathway?","choices":["The spinothalamic tracts","The posterior columns","The corticospinal tracts","The corticobulbar tract"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pain and temperature sensations are carried primarily by the spinothalamic tracts. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"Position sense (proprioception) and vibration are carried primarily by which pathway?","choices":["The spinothalamic tracts","The corticospinal tracts","The posterior columns","The spinocerebellar tracts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Position sense and vibration are carried primarily by the posterior columns. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"Recognizing a familiar object, such as a coin, placed in the hand with the eyes closed tests which discriminative sensation?","choices":["Graphesthesia","Two-point discrimination","Point localization","Stereognosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Stereognosis is the ability to recognize a familiar object by touch and manipulation alone with the eyes closed, a higher function of the sensory cortex. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 75"},{"q":"Recognizing a number drawn on the palm with the eyes closed tests which discriminative sensation?","choices":["Graphesthesia","Stereognosis","Extinction","Proprioception"],"answer":0,"exp":"Graphesthesia is the ability to identify a number drawn on the skin, such as the palm, with the eyes closed. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 76"},{"q":"Two-point discrimination on the finger pads is considered normal at approximately what distance?","choices":["About 5 centimeters","Less than 5 millimeters","About 2 centimeters","About 10 centimeters"],"answer":1,"exp":"Two-point discrimination on the finger pads is normally less than 5 millimeters, whereas on the foot it may be up to 5 centimeters. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 77"},{"q":"Touching both sides of the body simultaneously and asking the patient whether they feel one or two touches tests what?","choices":["Extinction (double simultaneous stimulation)","Vibratory sense with a tuning fork","The plantar (Babinski) response","Rapid alternating movements"],"answer":0,"exp":"Extinction is tested by touching each side individually and then both simultaneously; the patient should perceive both — this is double simultaneous stimulation. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 79"},{"q":"Vibratory sense is tested by placing a 128 Hertz tuning fork over which location?","choices":["A bony prominence such as a distal interphalangeal joint","The belly of a large muscle such as the biceps","The center of the palm away from any bone","A tendon such as the Achilles tendon"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vibratory sense is tested by placing a vibrating 128 Hertz tuning fork over a bony prominence, such as a distal interphalangeal joint of a finger or the joint of the first toe. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 82"},{"q":"The thumb and lateral wrist correspond to which dermatome?","choices":["C8","T4","C6","L5"],"answer":2,"exp":"The C6 dermatome corresponds to the lateral wrist and thumb (first digit). — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 71"},{"q":"The nipple line corresponds to which dermatome?","choices":["C6","T4","T10","S1"],"answer":1,"exp":"The T4 dermatome corresponds to the nipple line. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 71"},{"q":"The umbilicus corresponds to which dermatome?","choices":["T4","L1","C8","T10"],"answer":3,"exp":"The T10 dermatome corresponds to the umbilicus. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 71"},{"q":"The Romberg test assesses station by having the patient do what?","choices":["Walk heel-to-toe in a straight line","Stand with the feet together, first with eyes open then closed","Rapidly alternate hand movements","Touch the finger to the nose repeatedly"],"answer":1,"exp":"The Romberg test assesses station by having the patient stand with a narrow base, eyes open and then closed; excessive sway with the eyes closed suggests a position-sense problem. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 85"},{"q":"How is pronator drift tested?","choices":["The patient rapidly taps the foot on the floor","The patient walks placing heel to toe","The patient squeezes the examiner's fingers","The patient extends both arms forward, palms up, and closes the eyes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pronator drift is tested by having the patient extend both arms forward with palms up and close the eyes; a downward drift or pronation of one arm is abnormal. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 87"},{"q":"Which system integrates motor, vestibular, and sensory information to produce smooth, coordinated movement?","choices":["The spinothalamic system","The autonomic system","The corticobulbar system","The cerebellar system"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cerebellar system integrates information from the motor, vestibular, and sensory systems to facilitate smooth, rhythmic movements and steady posture. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 83"},{"q":"Hip flexion by the iliopsoas is primarily innervated by which nerve roots?","choices":["L2, L3, and L4 (femoral nerve)","C5 and C6 (axillary nerve)","S1 and S2 (sciatic nerve)","C7 and C8 (radial nerve)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Hip flexion by the iliopsoas is primarily innervated by the L2, L3, and L4 nerve roots through the femoral nerve. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 39"},{"q":"The triceps deep tendon reflex tests primarily which nerve roots?","choices":["C5 and C6","C6 and C7","L2 and L3","S1 and S2"],"answer":1,"exp":"The triceps deep tendon reflex tests primarily the C6 and C7 nerve roots. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 50"}]};
